{
  "rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underprediction: when the last 5–10 days show a persistent rise, the model systematically under-forecasts levels across the whole 30d horizon (often by ~+3 to +12 €/t, increasing with horizon). Apply an additive uplift that grows with lead time (small in days 1–3, larger by days 10–30).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the forecast path is too flat and reacts late to regime shifts (it keeps prices near ~70–73 while realized moves to ~78–84). Heuristic: increase forecast slope when recent momentum is positive (e.g., if 5d return > 3–4%, steepen the forward curve).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: short horizon (days 1–5) errors are modest; medium/long horizon (days 10–30) errors are materially more negative during rallies. Use a horizon-weighted correction (e.g., linear ramp of +bias from day 1 to day 30) rather than a constant shift.",
    "Insufficient spike follow-through: after a breakout/spike (new 20–30d high), the model assumes partial mean reversion, but realized prices continue trending higher. Rule: suppress mean-reversion terms for 1–3 weeks after a breakout; instead extrapolate momentum.",
    "Late-cycle pullback risk: once prices peak and begin to roll over (as in the final true outcomes), the model can remain too high/slow to reflect the downturn. Rule: if last 3–5 days show negative momentum after an extended rally, reduce the long-horizon uplift and allow faster downward adjustment."
  ]
}
